Course 4: Core strength (Detailed Research)

Category: Strength and conditioning
Duration: 15 min
Level: 1/5
What the Course Should Cover

This course focuses on core strength for stability and injury prevention, recommended 3 times per week. The core — comprising the muscles surrounding the trunk and hips that stabilise the spine and pelvis — is described by FIFA as "the epicentre for transferring movement throughout the body" and "the root of strength and stability". A strong core is essential for producing power, controlling external forces, and preventing injury in football, where players constantly push, pull, twist, turn, and change direction at speed.
Key Coaching Points
Understanding the Core's Role in Football

    The core is the kinetic link that transfers force with maximum efficiency between the upper and lower body

    It helps absorb the stress placed on the musculoskeletal system by the physical demands of the game

    Core stability underpins both stability and mobility, which facilitate efficient movement and dynamism on the pitch

    A strong core allows players to generate and transmit power through the entire kinetic chain

Training Principles

    Master simple exercises with near-perfect execution before progressing to more dynamic variations

    Quality over quantity — the focus is on control and technique, not speed or repetitions

    Consistency is key — 15 minutes, 3 times per week is the recommended dosage for youth players

    Progress gradually — only advance to the next exercise when excellent control has been achieved for an extended period

Key Technique Points for Each Exercise

    Prone isometric hold (plank): Engage core muscles, maintain a straight line from head to toe

    Plank with alternating arm reach: Try not to rotate towards the extended arm; maintain horizontal alignment

    Alternating Superman: Maintain horizontal alignment; engage the glute and spinal muscles of the non-raised leg for stability

    Glute-hamstring lifts: Strong extension of the bent leg with simultaneous flexion of the straight leg

    Side plank: Strong engagement of core, hips forward, neutral spine, feet in dorsiflexion

Common Mistakes to Address
Mistake	Why It Happens	Correction
Hips sag or rise during plank	Weak core engagement	Activate abdominal muscles, maintain straight line from head to toe
Rotating during alternating exercises	Lack of stability	"Try not to rotate towards the extended arm side"
Progressing too quickly to hard exercises	Eagerness to advance	Only progress when excellent control is achieved for an extended period
Holding breath during isometric holds	Tension and concentration	Breathe steadily throughout the hold
Neglecting side plank variations	Focus only on front plank	Side planks target hip abductors and obliques — essential for change of direction
Sample Exercises (from FIFA Training Centre)
Exercise 1: Prone Isometric Hold (Plank) — Foundation

    Target: Abdominal, transverse abdominal, obliques, hip flexors

    Execution: Engage core muscles while holding plank position. Keep a straight line from head to toe

    Progression: Only advance when excellent control and technique has been achieved for an extended period

Exercise 2: Plank, Alternating Arm Reach — Progression 1

    Target: Adds shoulder flexors, spinal extensors and rotators

    Execution: Try not to rotate towards the extended arm side as you alternate. Maintain horizontal alignment

    Progression: Advance to Exercise 3 once excellent control is achieved for multiple repetitions

Exercise 3: Alternating Superman — Progression 2

    Target: Adds hip extensors

    Execution: Maintain horizontal alignment as you alternate between legs. Engage the glute and spinal muscles of the non-raised leg to support stability. Hold each reach for 1-3 seconds

Exercise 4: Glute-Hamstring Lifts

    Target: Hip and spinal extensors, transverse abdominal, obliques, hip flexors

    Execution: Strong extension of the bent leg with simultaneous flexion of the straight leg. Hold briefly at the end of each raise

    Option: Place the foot of the working leg on a bench or chair, or use an unstable surface such as a physio ball

Exercise 5: Side Plank (Legs Together)

    Target: Hip abductors, spinal extensors and stabilisers, transverse abdominal, obliques

    Execution: Strong engagement of core, hips forward, keep a straight posture to maintain neutral spine. Keep feet in dorsiflexion

    Progression: Legs apart increases loading

Exercise 6: Side Plank (Legs Apart) — Progression

    Target: Increased loading of wide leg position

    Execution: From legs together, lift a leg into a wide position with a brief hold at the top (alternating)

    Advanced option: Add hip flexion, raising the knee to the elbow and lowering the elbow to the knee

Exercise 7: Seated Alternating Leg Raises

    Target: Hip flexors, abdominal, transverse abdominal, obliques, spinal extensors and stabilisers

    Execution: Keep back straight, head up, chest out, use arms to support posture. Lift each leg while keeping it straight, with a brief hold

Exercise 8: High Reach, On-the-Spot Marching

    Target: Abdominal, transverse abdominal, obliques, hip flexors, ankle, knee, hip and spinal stabilisers

    Execution: Strong engagement of core to maintain the pole steady in a horizontal position while marching

    Progression: Walking march, or increase resistance with a kettlebell, dumbbell, or weight plate overhead

Session Structure Suggestion (15 minutes)
Time	Activity	Focus
3 min	Prone isometric hold (plank)	Foundation stability
3 min	Plank with alternating arm reach	Anti-rotation control
3 min	Alternating Superman	Posterior chain stability
3 min	Side plank (both sides)	Lateral hip and core stability
3 min	Glute-hamstring lifts or seated leg raises	Hip and lower back integration

Note: This is a suggested structure. The FIFA Training Centre emphasises that players should only progress to more advanced exercises once excellent control and technique has been achieved in the simpler variations.
Assessment Indicators

A player has improved when they:

    Can hold a plank with perfect alignment for an extended period without sagging or rotating

    Maintains horizontal alignment during alternating arm or leg exercises without hip rotation

    Progresses through the exercise sequence only when control is excellent (not just completing repetitions)

    Demonstrates improved stability in change of direction and landing tasks during matches

